// The text "Copy prompt" puts on the clipboard: WIDGETS.md plus the request (keep in sync with WIDGETS.md).
export const WIDGET_PROMPT = "You are writing a widget for Nook, a personal notes app. Follow this specification exactly.\n\n# Nook widgets\n\nA **widget** is a small block with its own state – a timer, a counter, a habit tracker.\nIt is one JavaScript file that exports one plain object. Nook loads every `*.js` file in the\nworkspace folder **`.workspace/widgets/`** (Settings → Widgets → *Add the file* puts it there).\n\n## The whole format\n\n```js\nexport default {\n  id: 'water',                 // unique: a-z, 0-9, -\n  name: 'Water',               // shown in \"/\" → Widgets\n  icon: 'activity',            // a Nook icon name (any name from the icon picker)\n  description: 'Glasses of water today',\n\n  // starting state of a new widget (plain JSON)\n  defaults: () => ({ title: 'Water', goal: 8, count: 0, day: '', color: 'accent' }),\n\n  // rows of its settings menu (⋯ on the widget). Every change shows at once – there is no Save.\n  settings: [\n    { key: 'title', label: 'Name', type: 'text' },\n    { key: 'goal', label: 'Glasses per day', type: 'number', min: 1 },\n    { key: 'color', label: 'Color', type: 'color' },\n  ],\n\n  // draw it: return ONE element. Called again whenever you call ctx.redraw().\n  render(ctx) {\n    const d = ctx.data;\n    const today = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);\n    if (d.day !== today) ctx.set({ day: today, count: 0 });            // new day: start over\n    const add = () => { ctx.set({ count: d.count + 1 }); ctx.redraw(); };\n    return ctx.h('div', { class: 'w-col w-center' },\n      ctx.h('span', { class: 'w-label' }, d.title || 'Water'),\n      ctx.h('span', { class: 'w-big' }, `${d.count} / ${d.goal}`),\n      ctx.h('button', { class: 'w-btn primary', style: { background: ctx.color(d.color).tag }, onclick: add }, '+1 glass'));\n  },\n};\n```\n\n## Rules\n\n- **No `import`, no outside libraries.** Everything comes through `ctx`.\n- **State is `ctx.data`** – plain JSON. Change it only with `ctx.set({ ... })`; that merges and saves it\n  with the page. `set` does not redraw – call `ctx.redraw()` when the picture should change.\n- **Repeat work with `ctx.every(ms, fn)`**, never `setInterval` – Nook stops it when the widget goes away.\n- Return one element; Nook puts it in a card. Keep it small: a widget is a glance, not an app.\n\n## Design rules (so it looks like the rest of Nook)\n\n- **The top-right corner belongs to ⋯.** Put nothing there. Counts go next to the title\n  (`w-label strong` + `w-count`), extra buttons at the bottom of the widget.\n- **Every action is a real button** (`w-btn`, `w-btn primary`, `w-btn round` or a `w-chip`) – never plain clickable text.\n- **No settings inside the widget** (no text fields for configuration, no native date or colour inputs):\n  everything that configures the widget is a row in `settings`. Lists of things (links, names, dates) use the\n  `list` type, not one text field per line.\n- **Colours:** use a `color` setting with the default `'accent'`. Text, icons, bars and rings use `ctx.color(name).text`; filled buttons and other filled areas with text on them use `ctx.color(name).tag` (the same shade as option tags).\n  Don't hard-code colours; the background is chosen by the user (⋯ → Background) and Nook adapts greys to it.\n- Use the ready-made classes below and the theme's variables (`var(--fg)`, `var(--fg-muted)`, `var(--bg-hover)`, `var(--accent)`).\n\n## ctx – everything a widget can use\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `ctx.data` | the saved state |\n| `ctx.set(patch)` | merge into the state and save |\n| `ctx.redraw()` | call `render` again |\n| `ctx.every(ms, fn)` | run `fn` repeatedly while visible |\n| `ctx.h(tag, attrs, ...children)` | make an element. `attrs`: `class`, `style: { ... }`, `title`, `onclick`, any attribute. Children: text, elements, arrays, `null` |\n| `ctx.icon(name, size)` | a Nook icon (SVG) |\n| `ctx.color(name)` | `{ tag, bg, text }` of `'accent'` or a Nook colour: `text` for text, icons, bars; `tag` for filled buttons and labels; `bg` for soft backgrounds |\n| `ctx.colorNames` | the colour names (`accent` first) |\n| `ctx.openSettings()` | open the settings menu |\n| `ctx.pages()` | `[{ id, title, icon }]` of all pages |\n| `ctx.page(id)` / `ctx.openPage(id)` | read / open a page |\n| `ctx.openUrl(url)` | open a web address in a new tab |\n| `ctx.fetchJSON(url, cacheMinutes)` | GET JSON from an `https://` address (cached) |\n| `ctx.notify(text)` | a message on screen (+ system notification if allowed) |\n| `ctx.hour12` | true if the user writes times as 9:00 PM |\n| `ctx.phone` / `ctx.readonly` / `ctx.el` | phone layout? page locked? the widget's element |\n\n## Settings rows\n\nEvery row: `key`, `label`, optional `hint` (placeholder), optional `show: (data) => boolean` (only shown when true).\n\n| type | what it is |\n|---|---|\n| `text`, `number` (`min`, `max`, `step`), `textarea` | a field |\n| `toggle` | an on/off switch |\n| `select` | a choice: `options: [['value', 'Label'], …]` |\n| `color` | Nook's colours with swatches; `'accent'` is the app colour |\n| `date` | Nook's date picker, value `YYYY-MM-DD`; `optional: false` = a date is required |\n| `page` | a page of the workspace (its id) |\n| `timezone` | a searchable list of time zones (`Europe/Berlin` …) |\n| `section` | a small heading that groups the rows below it (no `key` needed) |\n| `list` | items to add, drag and remove: `item: [{ key, label, type }]` (types `text`, `number`, `select`, `date`, `page`, `timezone`, `link` = page or web address `page:<id>` / `https://…`, `emoji` = Nook's icon picker), `add: 'Add a …'`, `newItem: () => ({ … })`. The value is an array of objects. |\n\nNook adds **Background** to every widget's menu by itself. `background: 'yellow'` in the widget object\ngives new widgets a starting background.\n\n## Ready-made classes (they follow Nook's theme)\n\nLayout `w-col`, `w-row`, `w-wrap`, `w-between`, `w-center`, `w-gap`, `w-spacer` ·\nText `w-label` (+ `strong`), `w-count`, `w-big`, `w-sub`, `w-mono`, `w-quote` ·\nControls `w-btn` (+ `primary`, `round`), `w-chip` (+ `on`), `w-input` ·\nBar `w-bar` with an `<i style=\"width:40%;background:…\">` inside.\n\n## Asking an AI for a widget\n\nSettings → Widgets → **Copy prompt** copies this file plus a request line.\nPaste it into any chat AI, describe the widget after it, and save the answer as a `.js` file. Then\nSettings → Widgets → **Choose file…**. Widgets run as code in Nook: only add files you trust.\n\n\n---\nWrite ONE complete widget file (JavaScript, `export default { ... }`) for this request. Follow the design rules. Answer with the code only.\n\nMy widget: ";
